Texture parking lets the packed copies of restored textures go in its emergency band (Minato, patch 0019).

Why: a restored texture keeps its packed copy until it is written, so that parking it again only drops the GPU
storage. On an iPhone 15 Pro Max a visual novel's cycled frame sequence held 616 MiB of such copies. Near the limit
they are memory the process may need within the next frames, and a texture in use cannot be parked for them. Below
384 MiB available (the emergency band) a restore now lets its packed copy go, and a frame end that is still short
of memory after its parks drops the packed copies of the restored textures in use. Such a texture is read back and
packed again once it qualifies. ANGLEMetalParkingLine gains " unkept=<n>", the copies let go. The stress mode keeps
every copy as before.
Test: graphics/tests/angle_texture_parking.mm --in-use makes 240 frames, then draws 128 of them in every frame for
3 s; at the end at least 384 MiB stay available (patch 0018: 282-286 MiB, held by the copies; this patch: 561 MiB).
Upstream: no (Minato's memory policy).
